WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.100
The Yad Vashem, or World Holocaust Remembrance
Centre, commemorates non-Jews who took great

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.320
risk to save Jews during the Shoha with the
title of Righteous Among the Nations.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:18.960
Today’s protagonist is one of them.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.300
Cornelia ten Boom, known as Corrie, and her
close family, managed to save an estimated

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.760
800 lives during the Nazi occupation of the
Netherlands.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.500
During the War, her profound religious faith
and belief in God’s plans gave her the bravery

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.300
and resourcefulness to make it through situations
worthy of a spy story.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.110
But it was after the war that she achieved
her greatest accomplishment - forgiving her

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:44.930
former enemies and teaching the world to do
the same.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:52.770
The young watchmaker
Corrnelia Arnolda Joanna ten Boom was born

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:58.230
in Haarlem, near Amsterdam, on the 15th of
April 1892 in a devoutly religious family.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.440
Her father, Casper, was a watchmaker who ran
his business from their own home, the Beje

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.440
house.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.300
Corrie was the youngest of four siblings:
her sisters Betsie and Nollie, and her brother,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.300
Willem.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.000
Her mother, also named Cornelia, and her father
were members of the Dutch Baptist congregation

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.240
and a point of moral reference for the community.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.390
Inspire by their faith, the ten Booms would
offer shelter, food, even money to those in

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.390
need.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.630
They also maintained very good relationships
with the local Jewish community, whom they

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.610
considered “God’s ancient people”.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.620
Corrie was always a busy girl, helping those
in need, both in and outside her family.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:33.990
From a very young age she and Betsie organised
Bible clubs for the local youth and offered

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.300
tutoring to mentally disabled children.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.860
When here elderly aunts moved in, she still
found the time to look after them.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.930
To make space for the aunties, Casper bought
the house next to the Beje.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.090
The levels of the two buildings did not match,
and there was an alleyway between them, but

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:51.530
the two houses had to be connected: this resulted
in a very odd layout with winding staircases

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.650
and empty spaces between walls.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.280
We will soon see why this is important.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.500
In her late teens, Corrie met and fell in
love for the first time with a young man.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:04.360
She was expecting him to propose, but his
mother did not approve of the union, deeming

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.140
the ten Booms of a lower social status.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.780
Her heart was broken.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.039
She vowed to dedicate all of her love to Jesus.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.010
But, as she pointed out:
“Jesus has taken care so for me that I have

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.120
never become a frustrated old spinster”.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.780
In 1918 mother ten Boom became bed ridden
from a cerebral haemorrhage.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.950
This added to Corrie’s long list of commitments,
but her energy never failed her.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.950
She dedicated herself to all housework and
even to managing the family business.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.900
She studied watchmaking, becoming the first
Dutch licensed woman watchmaker.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.560
Unlike Casper, who wasn’t very business
minded, Corrie had a keen commercial sense

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.340
and with her management of the shop the family
did better financially.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.099
Unfortunately, after 3 years of illness, Corrie’s
mother died in 1921.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:47.819
The elderly aunts had also passed away shortly
before.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:52.020
With an empty house and plenty of spare time,
Corrie and her family took the occasion to

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.010
help once more those in need: first, the Beje
started welcoming the children left in the

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.290
Netherlands by Missionaries travelling to
the Indonesian colonies.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.660
Then, over much of the 1930s, Corrie established
a youth club for teenagers, where she taught

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:09.590
religious studies, performing arts and handicrafts.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:14.760
Invasion
Unfortunately for her – and for the Netherlands

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.070
– and for Europe – over the border in
Germany, another type of youth club was gaining

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.819
ground[TA1] .
Europe and the whole world were heading towards

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:25.590
another war, but most people in the Netherlands,
including the Prime Minister, believed that

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.260
Germany would respect Dutch neutrality, as
was the case in WWI.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.569
What they didn’t know is that General Von
Manstein’s plan to invade France relied

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:39.660
on an initial attack through the Netherlands,
to divert Allied forces from the real, main

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.420
invading force entering Belgium via the Ardennes
forest.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.069
And so, after conquering Poland, Denmark and
Norway, the German military started to turn

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:50.070
their attention towards the Western allied
powers.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.530
The ‘Phoney War’ was about to give way
to the actual one.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.790
On the 10th of May 1940 the Wehrmacht launched
a combined attack on the Netherlands, supported

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.680
by frequent bombing raids on the major Dutch
cities.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.700
One night, Corrie was woken up by the noise
of bombers flying over Haarlem.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.720
She stood up and went into the kitchen, where
Betsie, also unable to sleep, offered her

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:09.720
some tea.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.090
When Corrie returned to her room, she hurt
her hand over something sharp: it was a piece

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.780
of shrapnel from an exploding bomb nearby.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.569
The morning after she pointed out to Betsie
that if she had not drunk tea with her the

00:04:19.569 --> 00:04:21.599
night before, she could have been killed.

00:04:21.599 --> 00:04:24.919
The older sister remarked
“There are no ifs with God, being in the

00:04:24.919 --> 00:04:29.820
centre of his will is our only safety”
Despite a desperate resistance, German divisions

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.559
quickly overrun the country.

00:04:31.559 --> 00:04:35.360
On the 13th of May the Dutch Government and
the Royal Family were evacuated to Britain.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.360
On the 14th the Country capitulated
The Nazi occupation was harsh from the beginning,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.080
with the Gestapo starting to round up Jews
and Resistance fighters with the help of local

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.059
collaborators, as well as confiscating all
radios.

00:04:48.059 --> 00:04:52.189
The ten Booms managed to keep a clandestine
wireless, listening to messages of hope coming

00:04:52.189 --> 00:04:55.719
from the BBC and from to the Dutch Queen in
exile in London.

00:04:55.719 --> 00:04:58.360
They also listened to some of Hitler’s propaganda
speeches.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.669
As Corrie remembered
“It was the voice of a demon”

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:05.759
Casper ten Boom was a pillar of the Haarlem
community and so very soon his family was

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:09.389
approached by resistance fighters and Jewish
citizens asking for help.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:13.379
Casper, Corrie and Betsie faced a dilemma:
their faith compelled them to protect Jews

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:18.249
but being part of the resistance implied taking
a political stance which was against their

00:05:18.249 --> 00:05:24.930
beliefs.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.800
The Hiding Place
The doubts were wiped out by two events.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.199
First, Corrie found out that her brother Willem
and his son Kik had already entered the clandestine

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:35.840
resistance and were already offering shelter
to Jews on the run.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.869
Even before the invasion, Willem had used
a nursing home he was managing as a haven

00:05:39.869 --> 00:05:42.290
for German Jews who had fled persecution.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:46.810
Then, Casper had heard about a local Pastor
who had refused to hide a Jewish orphan boy.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:52.349
He decided to welcome the boy into the Beje,
the first of many lives, up to 800, that the

00:05:52.349 --> 00:05:54.729
ten Booms would save during the war.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.930
Corrie had a knack for organising people around
her.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.919
She soon put this talent to fruition when
she recruited 80 of the members of her various

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:07.020
youth and Bible groups to what became a very
effective clandestine organisation.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.620
Taking advantage of the uncommon layout of
the Beje, and with the help of an architect

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.259
in the resistance, Corrie had a secret room
- a Hiding Place - built behind a fake wall

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:16.379
in her own bedroom.

00:06:16.379 --> 00:06:18.619
The place could hide 6 people at the time.

00:06:18.619 --> 00:06:20.909
She also had installed a system of buzzers
around the house.

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:24.759
In case of danger, a member of the family
would ring the buzzers, allowing the refugees

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.449
to rush to the secret room where they would
stand still and silent.

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:32.530
When the coast was clear, the refugees would
be sent to a one of a vast network of safe

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.970
houses around the Country until they could
flee abroad.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:39.409
In one occasion, the ten Booms heard that
the Nazis had set their sights on a Jewish

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:40.409
orphanage.

00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:43.259
Dozens of babies were facing certain death.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:45.029
Corrie organised a daring raid.

00:06:45.029 --> 00:06:49.449
Among the people she helped, there were also
German soldiers, deserters who did not want

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:51.330
to carry out further atrocities.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.919
The ten Booms would give them civilian clothing
and keep their uniforms.

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:58.719
In this case, Corrie handed out the uniforms
to members of her organisation.

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:03.050
In this disguise, they simply entered the
orphanage and stole 100 hundred babies!

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.949
As the number of people they helped increased,
the family had to solve the problem of feeding

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:07.949
them.

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:11.569
Food was rationed through a system of cards,
of which the ten Booms only held three.

00:07:11.569 --> 00:07:15.259
By luck, or divine providence, the local man
in charge of the ration cards was one Fred

00:07:15.259 --> 00:07:19.729
Koonstra, whose mentally disabled daughter
had been attending Corrie’s church services

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:20.789
for 20 years.

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:25.319
Corrie was not sure if she could trust him,
but she boldly asked for 100 ration cards.

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:27.740
To her surprise, Fred agreed immediately.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:32.270
But how could he justify 100 missing cards
to his Nazi supervisor?

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.659
In a stroke of genius, Fred staged a realistic
robbery, even asking a friend to beat him

00:07:36.659 --> 00:07:38.339
up to make it more plausible!

00:07:38.339 --> 00:07:42.619
The clandestine activities around the Beje
increased with time, despite the threat of

00:07:42.619 --> 00:07:47.270
the Gestapo ever present, and a police station
only a few blocks away.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.679
As Corrie feared that their phone was tapped,
she devised a secret code.

00:07:50.679 --> 00:07:54.509
The sentence “I need a watch to be repaired”
meant that another person needed hiding.

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:59.409
The watchmaker shop was a perfect front for
people coming and going at all time, after

00:07:59.409 --> 00:08:00.409
all.

00:08:00.409 --> 00:08:03.759
Casper devised another code: a triangular
advertising sign on the shop window meant

00:08:03.759 --> 00:08:12.569
that it was safe to come in, if placed back
to front it meant that it was better to wait.

00:08:12.569 --> 00:08:17.800
The arrest
In February 1944 a man called Jan Vogel came

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.529
asking for a different type of help.

00:08:19.529 --> 00:08:23.119
His wife, accused of being in the resistance,
was in custody.

00:08:23.119 --> 00:08:28.520
A sympathetic policeman had promised to free
her, but had requested a bribe of 600 guilders.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.870
Jan did not have the money and asked the ten
Booms for a loan.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:36.410
The following day, at 5am, the ten Booms were
holding a Bible reading with many family members

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:39.800
and friends from the community attending,
alongside six refugees.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:40.840
The doorbell rang.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.849
When Betsie opened the door, she was pushed
aside by a Nazi officer.

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:46.550
Other soldiers burst in, led by Vogel.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:48.240
He was a collaborator.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.270
Betsie was able to hit one of the buzzers,
and the six refugees - four Jews and two resistance

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:53.820
fighters – run to Corrie’s bedroom.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.770
While the soldiers were negotiating the strange
layout of the house, making their way upwards,

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.520
Corrie was able to hide the refugees behind
the fake wall.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.790
She later commented that the uncommon floorplans
of the Beje were another sign of God’s providence,

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.500
which had come to help her in her predicament.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.680
The Nazis accused her of hiding Jews, hitting
her repeatedly, but she kept her mouth tight.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.950
The soldiers searched the house but could
find the secret room.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:18.160
One of them was heard saying
“If there is a secret room here, the Devil

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.530
himself built it!”

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:24.060
Corrie, Betsie and Casper were arrested alongside
other 30 friends and taken to Scheveningen

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.060
prison.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:28.990
As Corrie was ill with pleurisy she was put
in solitary confinement to prevent contagion

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:33.300
One day in her lonely jail, Corrie received
a letter from her sister Nollie, who was still

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.300
free.

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:35.300
The letter bore very sad news.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.330
Casper, the elderly father ten Boom, had died
ten days after imprisonment.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.970
Corrie had not seen him since the day they
had arrived in Scheveningen.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.780
It was devastating for Corrie to learn that
her father who had been the moral guiding

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.820
light to her and to so many people in Haarlem
was no more.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.780
Despite the grief, there were some good news,
too.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:55.140
Corrie noticed that the address on the envelope
slanted towards the stamp.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.890
Curious, she unpeeled it carefully and found
a secret message underneath it

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:03.560
“All the watches are safe” – a code
phrase meaning that all of their Jewish guests

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.190
were out of the hiding place and still alive.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:09.150
Corrie had little time to grieve as she was
summoned for interrogation.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:12.710
The first hearing was with a German officer,
a Lieutenant Rahms.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:16.620
The officer was polite, albeit dismissive
of Corrie’s activism in supporting and protecting

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.250
the mentally disabled, seen as ‘undesirables’
by the III Reich.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.610
Caring for them was a waste of energy as
“one normal person is worth all the ‘half-wits’

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.080
in the World!”.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.070
To which Corrie replied
“In God’s eyes, one ‘half-wit’ might

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:30.480
be worth more than a watchmaker.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:31.480
Or a Lieutenant.”

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:32.590
And that’s how you put a Nazi in his place.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:36.450
The next interrogator was a Dutch judge, collaborating
with German authorities.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.380
In Corrie’s own words, her “faith touched
him, and he became a friend”.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.270
But this friend still had to do his job.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:46.620
The judge produced some documents found at
the Beje: a list of names and addresses which

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.760
would immediately incriminate her remaining
family members and resistance friends who

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:51.760
were still at large.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:55.680
The judge remarked how dangerous those papers
were to the safety of the organisation she

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.680
had set up.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:01.790
Then, without adding a word, he simply turned
to his stove, opened the door and threw them

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.230
into the flames.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.240
After 3 months Corrie was finally taken out
of confinement and reunited with her beloved

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:08.240
sister Betsie.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:12.130
There was no hope to be set free, though,
as they and all other prisoners were loaded

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:16.660
onto cattle trains and sent to the Dutch labour
camp of Vught, where they were forced to make

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.010
radios for the Luftwaffe.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.750
Then the 6th of June 1944 arrived: D-Day.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:25.380
As the Allies pushed their way towards Flanders,
the Nazis decided to empty their camps in

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.050
the Netherlands and relocate the prisoners
in Germany.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:35.170
Corrie and Betsie ended up in Ravensbruck,
the infamous camp for women prisoners.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:40.620
Ravensbruck
The experience in the concentration camp was

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:41.620
just hellish.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:45.370
Corrie, Betsie, and the other prisoners were
subjected to endless days of labour, starvation,

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.250
illness and abuse by the guards.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:51.090
And yet, every night, the two sisters would
find the time and energy to organise prayer

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:54.250
groups in their barracks, with the help of
smuggled Bible.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:59.220
Every night, this would give the incarcerated
women some peace, faith and hope.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.020
And every night Corrie and Betsie feared an
inspection from the guards, well knowing that

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.450
Bibles and prayer were forbidden in the camp.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.750
But, in another stroke of luck – or divine
intervention – word had spread that their

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.730
barracks were flea-infested and so the wardens
kept at a distance.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:15.460
In the late Autumn Betsie fell ill.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:19.610
During this time, she discussed with Corrie
a vision she had, a plan to heal the wounds

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.110
of the suffering in post-War Europe.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.630
First, they were going to set up a sort of
rehabilitation centre in the Netherlands to

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.920
provide support for those who had suffered
during the war.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.590
And the centre had to be opened also to Nazi
collaborators, so as to reform them and reintegrate

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:34.850
them into society.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:38.310
Then, Corrie and Betsie would then proceed
to help the German people, as it was clear

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.990
that their Country was going to be devastated.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:45.880
Betsie even had a mental image of a former
concentration camp, painted in green and converted

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.250
into a welcome centre.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.200
Betsie would not live to see her vision.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.210
She died on the 16th of December 1944.

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:56.600
Now alone, and losing hope, on the morning
of the 28th of December, Corrie was summoned

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.470
to the commander’s office, asked to fill
in some forms and was simply … released.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:06.100
She later discovered that this was due to
a clerical error and that only one week later

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:10.560
all the women of her age group had been taken
to the gas chambers.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:18.250
Forgive, not forget
The War had taken a huge toll on the ten Booms.

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.780
Casper, Betsie and brother Willem were dead.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.970
Willem’s son, Kik, was missing in action.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.640
Corrie, she would not surrender, though.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:28.850
She immediately resumed her work with mentally
disabled children, but most of all, she carried

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:32.520
out Betsie’s vision, her plan for reconciliation.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.380
As she was setting up her home to rehabilitate
former collaborators, she received a letter

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:39.740
from the man who had betrayed her and her
family – Jan Vogel.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:43.570
Awaiting a death sentence, Jan had written
to Corrie asking her if she could forgive

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:44.570
him.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:45.970
She could, and she did.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:50.880
This was the first of many acts of forgiveness
for her old enemies and tormentors.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:54.210
Continuing Betsie’s vision, Corrie moved
to Germany and succeeded in gaining management

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:57.190
of an ex concentration camp in Darmstadt.

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:00.450
As per her sister’s wishes, she had all
the building s painted in bright green and

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.320
flowers planted around the houses.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:07.230
The old site of death became a housing centre
for German families left homeless by the Allied

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:08.360
bombing raids.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.520
Corrie also started to tour churches from
the Netherlands to Germany, giving talks about

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.420
her experiences in Ravensbruck.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:18.620
On one such occasion she noticed a man standing
at the back of the room who could not meet

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:19.620
her gaze.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.860
She then recognised him: he was a former guard
at Ravensbruck, one who had been particularly

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:24.860
cruel to her sister.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.820
He had become a Christian and was now looking
for forgiveness.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:29.260
He stretched out his hand.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:33.300
Corrie initially could not accept his hand,
the memories of the man’s cruelties still

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:34.300
strong.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:38.700
But she remembered from the Bible that
“Hatred means murder in God’s eyes”

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.940
She was able to forgive the former guard.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:45.320
This act would inspire her to continue her
tours, relating her experience and preaching

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:50.150
the power of forgiving your enemies
This was a powerful and courageous idea.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:54.630
One cannot deny that the Nuremberg trials
were what Nazi war criminals deserved and

00:14:54.630 --> 00:15:00.150
yet the idea of private forgiveness can coexist
with the notion of public justice – as philosopher

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:05.430
Paul Ricoeur put it, forgiveness is not amnesty:
“Amnesty is organised forgetting and it

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:10.480
has nothing to do with the pacification that
forgiveness can bring between two consciences”

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:13.610
[TA2]
In the late 1960s Corrie moved to the US to

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:16.120
spread her messages across congregations of
all faiths.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:20.041
She was penniless and lived frugally, all
the publicity she could afford was to personally

00:15:20.041 --> 00:15:24.050
go from one church to another and ask if the
community was interested in hearing her story.

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:29.100
Little by little, people started to take notice
of this elderly lady, her oratory style and

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.100
her values.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.970
Her admirers suggested that she wrote a book
about her experience.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:38.620
The Hiding Place was published in 1971, followed
by a film adaptation in 1975.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:42.040
Corrie continued to receive more and more
invitations to speak worldwide, but she had

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:44.890
to slow down engagements due to her declining
health.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:49.210
One morning in 1978, Corrie did not wake up
at dawn as usual.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:53.490
Her assistant and friend Pam Rosewell Moore
went to wake her up, as it was uncommon for

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.230
Corrie to oversleep in the mornings.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:16:00.170
Pam found out that during the night Corrie
had had a cerebral-vascular stroke, from which

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:01.170
she did not recover.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:05.720
For the following 5 years she was paralysed,
unable to talk, read and write.

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:12.540
Finally, on the day of her 91st Birthday,
15th of April 1981, Corrie ten Boom died peacefully.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:16.870
According to Jewish tradition, only those
who are specially blessed by God are granted

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:19.650
the privilege of dying on the same date of
their birthday.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.850
Well, I hope you find Corrie ten Boom’s
life story interesting and inspiring.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:27.360
We would like to hear your thoughts, what
do you believe is more morally just, righteous

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:28.360
punishment or forgiveness?

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:31.230
Please comment below and as usual, thank you
for watching.

